# What Foundry may do here without asking again.
#
# One line per standing grant. `grade` is reading and running — clone it, run its gates, record what
# passed. `deliver` is proposing — push a branch, open a pull request. Neither is merging.
#
# Read from the run's base commit, never the working tree. A worker can edit this file; editing it
# grants nothing until a human commits it and a later run reads it.
#
# **Merging is not a grant floor can withhold.** Anything that can run `gh` can merge, whatever this
# file says — floor is not a security boundary and says so.
#
# **Nothing else withholds it here either.** This used to claim Foundry runs under an identity
# lacking that permission, and that the provider refuses. Measured 2026-08-25: the identity `gh`
# holds on this machine has `admin` on this repository, and over a hundred pull requests have been
# merged under it. The provider refuses nothing and never has here.
#
# What holds is the operator's word and this file — both records, neither a control. #156 owns the
# identity that could make a refusal real.
#
# Accepted 2026-08-19 by chriss.attard@gmail.com:
#
#   "Foundry may read and test attac-t/the-foundry, and may push branches and open pull requests
#    on it, without asking again."

grade    https://github.com/attac-t/the-foundry.git
deliver  https://github.com/attac-t/the-foundry.git

# `offer` makes an issue eligible for a pass: `offer <label> <who may put it on>`. A pass takes the
# oldest such label first. The label means something only from a hand named here, and floor never
# puts one on itself.
#
# Added by a worker at the repository owner's word, 26 September 2026, and not by the owner's own
# hand. The words: "you have browser access and you can do it yourself, the 'offer' and foundry tag
# thing."

offer    foundry:go  attac-t
